<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>
        <a href="/manage/audio-clips">Audio Clips</a> are short recordings played over the kiosk music, such as
        a closing-time call, a birthday jingle or a safety message. A clip can be an uploaded recording, or an
        announcement you type for a voice to say. The music dips while a clip plays and comes
        back afterwards. Admins, Managers and people with the CanPlayAudioClips role can play them.
    </FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="Adding a clip (managers)">
        <ol>
            <li>Select <strong>Add clip</strong> and upload an MP3, WAV or M4A file (up to 25 MB and 5 minutes), or pick one
                already in Files.</li>
            <li>Give it a <strong>Name</strong> and choose where it <strong>Plays at</strong>: one location, or
                <strong>Every location</strong>.</li>
            <li>Set the clip volume. Turn on <strong>Change how the music dips</strong> to set the music level
                while it plays and how quickly it fades down and back up.</li>
        </ol>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Typing an announcement (managers)">
        <ol>
            <li>Select <strong>Add clip</strong>, and under <strong>Audio</strong> choose <strong>Type an announcement</strong>.</li>
            <li>Type <strong>What to say</strong>, up to 500 characters, for example "Red team, this is your two minute warning."</li>
            <li>Choose a <strong>Voice</strong>. All of them are British: English, Scottish and Northern, men and women.</li>
            <li>Move <strong>Speed</strong> and <strong>Pitch</strong> if you like. A deep, slightly slow voice works well for
                laser tag; a brighter, quicker one for parties.</li>
            <li>Select <strong>Preview</strong> to hear it, then <strong>Add</strong>.</li>
        </ol>
        <FluentText>
            Announcements show an <strong>Announcement</strong> tag in the list, with their words underneath. They play,
            schedule and work in automation rules exactly like an uploaded clip. To change the words or the voice, select
            the clip's edit button; it's spoken again when you save, and the kiosks fetch the new version.
        </FluentText>
        <HelpNote>
            The words are spoken on the Lanyard server (nothing is sent to another company) and kept as the clip's audio,
            so don't type a child's name into an announcement. Deleting an announcement deletes its audio too.
        </HelpNote>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Playing a clip now">
        <FluentText>
            Select <strong>Play</strong> on a clip, tick the kiosks, and confirm. You can only play clips on
            kiosks at your own location, and a kiosk needs its location set to play clips.
            <strong>Recently played</strong> shows how each play went: Sent, Getting ready, Playing, Played,
            Stopped, Offline, or Needs update (the kiosk needs a newer version).
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Schedules">
        <FluentText>
            A schedule plays a clip by itself on every kiosk at a location, on the days you tick. Choose
            <strong>Before closing</strong>, <strong>After opening</strong> or <strong>At a set time</strong> (UK time).
            Before closing and after opening use the location's opening hours, so set those on Companies &amp;
            Locations first. A late close after midnight counts as the day before.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Announcements in a game">
        <FluentText>
            To announce a moment in a laser tag game, such as a two minute warning, add an automation rule on
            <a href="/automation">Automation</a>: choose the trigger <strong>Time remaining drops below</strong> with
            <strong>Seconds remaining</strong> set to 120, and the action <strong>Play Audio Clip</strong> with your announcement.
            Kiosks download the clips their rules play ahead of time, so the announcement starts right on the moment.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
